INT. VENETIAN MASQUERADE BALL - GRAND SALON - NIGHT

1790s. Chandeliers hang like frozen waterfalls. Marble floors mirror the candlelight. Gilded masks drift in the crowd, each hiding a face. A string quartet plays a dance no one is dancing.

CONTESSA ISABELLA ROSSELLINI, 40s, in a silver gown and a black velvet half-mask, stands against a pillar, a glass of wine untouched in her hand. Her eyes follow every mask in the room.

GIOVANNI CASSINI, 30s, a young poet in a plum coat and a gold mask shaped like a sun, lingers at her elbow. He has the air of a man who has rehearsed a speech and forgotten it.

GIOVANNI
Contessa.

ISABELLA
Signor Cassini.

GIOVANNI
I did not think you would come.

ISABELLA
Nor did I.

GIOVANNI
And yet.

ISABELLA
And yet.

She turns her glass slowly in her hand. A servant passes with a tray. Neither takes a thing.

GIOVANNI
Your husband is abroad?

ISABELLA
My husband is always abroad.

GIOVANNI
How fortunate.

ISABELLA
(softly)
Is it?

She looks at him then, properly, for the first time. His fingers close and open at his side.

GIOVANNI
I have written something.

ISABELLA
You are always writing something.

GIOVANNI
This is different.

ISABELLA
They are all different.

GIOVANNI
This one is about a woman at a ball who does not dance.

ISABELLA
(very quietly)
How does it end?

GIOVANNI
I have not decided.

The quartet strikes a new measure. Across the room, a tall man in a raven mask parts the crowd with the ease of a prow. He moves toward them in a straight line. Isabella's glass stops turning.

COUNT ALVISE ROSSELLINI, 50s, in the raven mask and a coat of midnight blue, arrives before them and bows. Beneath the beak, his mouth is a thin smile.

ALVISE
My dear. I came home early.

ISABELLA
So I see.

ALVISE
And who is your friend?

GIOVANNI
(bowing)
Giovanni Cassini, Excellency. A humble poet.

ALVISE
Poets are never humble. That is why they are poets.

He takes the glass from Isabella's hand. Drinks it. Returns it.

ALVISE (CONT'D)
I trust you were enjoying yourselves.

ISABELLA
We were talking.

ALVISE
How charming.

He turns to the room. Raises his voice, light and carrying.

ALVISE (CONT'D)
Ladies and gentlemen. A toast!

The quartet falters. The crowd turns. Isabella sets her jaw. Giovanni's mask suddenly looks absurd.

ALVISE (CONT'D)
To fidelity.

Glasses rise. Murmurs of agreement. Isabella lifts hers last.

ISABELLA
(under her breath)
To the masks.
